      "id": 75,
      "title": "Galapagos corals suggest climate change drives stronger El Niños",
      "url": "https://newscientist.com/article/2586758-galapagos-corals-suggest-climate-change-drives-stronger-el-ninos/",
      "summary": "Coral records from the Galapagos Islands indicate El Niños have been gathering strength over the past 40 years, in line with rising global temperatures",
      "content": "Galapagos corals suggest climate change drives stronger El Niños | New Scientist Advertisement Subscribe now Researchers sample corals growing near the Galapagos Islands NOAA A super El Niño that is gathering strength in the Pacific Ocean may have been made worse by climate change, according to new analysis of coral records that suggest El Niños have become more intense as global temperatures have climbed. The impacts of El Niño – droughts, floods and storms – are already exacerbated by rising global temperatures, because a warmer atmosphere intensifies both rainfall and droughts. But scientists have long struggled to untangle the effect of climate change on the El Niño Southern Oscillation (ENSO) cycle, which drives El Niño events. Part of the difficulty is that detailed observational records only began in the 1980s. A much longer timescale than that is needed to tease out a trend from natural variability. Advertisement Read more The last-ditch plan to save coral reefs from utter destruction Julia Cole at the University of Michigan and her colleagues hope corals can provide the answer. They collected living and dead corals from around the Galapagos Islands in the eastern Pacific ocean, where El Niños first develop. They used uranium and thorium dating to identify the age of the corals, then analysed the chemistry of their skeletons to track changes to sea temperatures during their lifetime. In that way, they built a time series of sea surface temperatures in the eastern Pacific spanning the past 1000 years. El Niño and La Niña events are measured by changes to sea surface temperatures in the Pacific, so by building this time series, Cole and her colleagues could track the fluctuating ENSO cycle through the centuries. Free newsletter Sign up to The Earth Edition Sign up to newsletter “The chemistry of the coral skeleton preserves a record of the environment in which it grew, and certain aspects of the chemistry are especially sensitive to temperature changes,” says Cole. The team found that, over the past 40 years, variability in the ENSO cycle has increased by 36.5 per cent compared with the pre-industrial period. This increase in variability has been driven largely by more intense El Niño events, the analysis found. The corals collected by the team covered about half the 1000-year time period. Nevertheless, Cole is confident in the overall trend identified. “I think we show pretty clearly that the increase in variability in ENSO is tracking global temperature closely,” she says. Super El Niño could lead to mega CO2 emissions from fires in Indonesia Intense fires are already burning in Indonesia, and there could be much worse to come as the Super El Niño ramps up The findings add to an emerging body of evidence that suggests the ENSO cycle is changing in response to rising temperatures. “The evidence is better than 50-50 that climate change is affecting El Niño,” says Mark Cane at Columbia University in New York, whose own work used tree ring data to identify an increase in El Niño activity in recent years. Although the research does not include the recent El Niños in 2015, 2023 and 2026, Cole says they are consistent with the trend identified by the corals. When the current El Niño peaks at the end of this year, sea surface temperatures in the east-central Pacific Ocean are projected to reach 4°C above normal, a 60 per cent increase over the old record. Michael McPhaden at the US National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) agrees. “If the current El Niño turns out to be as strong as we expect, that past decade will have witnessed three major El Niños – a sequence not seen before in the instrumental record dating back to the late 19th century,” he says. “It will add to the mounting evidence that climate change is amplifying the ENSO cycle… which is a concern considering the far-reaching global impacts that ENSO has on humans and natural systems.” Journal Reference: Science DOI: 10.1126/science.ady2660 Topics: Climate change / Coral More from New Scientist Explore the latest news, articles and features Environment Octopuses released into Adriatic Sea to tackle invasive crabs News Environment Glaciers in the Alps are on track for record-breaking ice loss News Environment 5 graphs that show how the record-breaking El Niño will affect the world News Environment Are mushroom-growing kits seeding a biodiversity crisis across Europe? Features Popular articles Trending New Scientist articles Octopuses released into Adriatic Sea to tackle invasive crabs Richard Feynman’s 80-year-old quantum postulate has now been validated Exclusive video of world’s first echidna puggle to be born at a university Einstein thought time dilation was both real and not – he was right From back pain to cancer, how the interstitium could transform medicine Where and when to see 2027’s ‘solar eclipse of the century’ ‘Thinking about the edges of space-time can help build a better world’ Doctors are finally learning to manage antidepressant withdrawal Healed fang wound found on 520-million-year-old apex predator Nanoparticles ease Alzheimer’s by making neurons from other cells Advertisement",
